# MoonlightWeb — access to /dev/uinput, for the virtual gamepad.
#
# A gamepad is the one input that cannot be injected with an API call: games do
# not read input events, they enumerate devices, so a real device has to exist.
# On Linux the kernel provides that through /dev/uinput — no driver to install
# and nothing to sign, unlike Windows — but the node is root-only by default.
#
# Without this rule MoonlightWeb would have to run as root for a controller to
# work, which is a wildly disproportionate price for one feature. With it, the
# server runs as an ordinary user and the pad still appears.
#
# What each part does:
#
#   static_node=uinput  creates the node with these permissions BEFORE the
#                       uinput module is loaded, so the very first session does
#                       not have to wait for a modprobe to have happened.
#   TAG+="uaccess"      hands access to whoever is logged in at the seat, which
#                       is how a desktop install runs (as that user). This is
#                       systemd-logind's own mechanism, and the same one Steam
#                       and Sunshine rely on for exactly this device.
#   GROUP="input"       covers the headless install instead, where the server
#                       runs as a system user with no seat: add that user to the
#                       `input` group and the pad works there too.
#
# Nothing here grants anything to a remote visitor. It is the SERVER process on
# this machine that gains the right to create a pad; what a remote client can
# do with it is decided by MoonlightWeb's own input policy, unchanged.
KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput", TAG+="uaccess", MODE="0660", GROUP="input"
